Defining Wave Types
------------------------

### Entry Point Wave Type

This is the *WaveType* used to process a Wave generated anywhere into the application.


```java
WaveType DO_LOAD_EVENTS = WBuilder.waveType("LOAD_EVENTS")
    .items(EditorWaves.EVENTS_FILE)
    .returnAction("EVENTS_LOADED")
    .returnItem(EditorWaves.EVENTS);
```

- Full entry contract on the service interface — one constant defines call + return waves.
- `.items(...)` — input parameters; `.returnAction` / `.returnItem` — completion contract.


This **WaveType** uses only one **WaveItem** to store the file that must be loaded.
WaveItem wrap the type of the object we want to use, thus it's possible to check that API contract isn't broken.

Minimal entry `WaveType` on a service interface:

```java
WaveType DO_LOAD = WBuilder.waveType("LOAD")
    .items(MyWaves.INPUT_FILE)
    .returnAction("LOADED")
    .returnItem(MyWaves.RESULT_LIST);
```

- `waveType("LOAD")` — action name; handler method will be `doLoad(...)`.
- `.items(...)` — input parameters carried by the call wave.
- `.returnAction("LOADED")` — name of the completion wave (`doLoaded(...)` handler).
- `.returnItem(...)` — typed slot for the value returned from the task method.

### Exit Point Wave Type

The return Wave Type is automatically created with by the using the _returnAction_ value with _returnItem_ as **WaveItem**.
The code above will generate a **WaveType** that uses only one WaveItem to store the list of events loaded from the given file.


```java
// Generated return type — listen with:
listen(LoadEdtFileService.DO_LOAD_EVENTS.returnWaveType());

public void doEventsLoaded(final List<JRebirthEvent> events, final Wave wave) {
    // handler for return action EVENTS_LOADED
}
```

- `.returnWaveType()` — accessor on the entry `WaveType` for the auto-generated completion wave.
- Handler name comes from `returnAction` (`EVENTS_LOADED` → `doEventsLoaded`).


Task Registration
-------------------

Each Task requires to be registered in order to generate the right WaveType that wrap the return value.
	
This registration must be done into the JRebirth's Component **void initService()** method like this:


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.service.impl;

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.annotation.PriorityLevel;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.concurrent.Priority;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.facade.JRebirthEvent;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.module.Register;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.facade.JRebirthEventBase;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.log.JRebirthMarkers;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.service.DefaultService;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.service.ServiceUtility;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.service.LoadEdtFileService;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

/**
 * The class <strong>LoadEdtFileServiceImpl</strong> is the basic implementation used to load a log file.
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 */
@Register(value = LoadEdtFileService.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Low)
public class LoadEdtFileServiceImpl extends DefaultService implements LoadEdtFileService {

    /** The class logger. */
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(LoadEdtFileServiceImpl.class);

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void initService() {

        listen(DO_LOAD_EVENTS);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    @Priority(PriorityLevel.High)
    public List<JRebirthEvent> doLoadEvents(final File selecteFile, final Wave wave) {

        final List<JRebirthEvent> eventList = new ArrayList<>();

        updateMessage(wave, "Parsing events");

        // Get number of line to calculate the task progression
        final int totalLines = ServiceUtility.countFileLines(selecteFile);

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(selecteFile));) {
            int processedLines = 0;

            String strLine = br.readLine();

            // Read File Line By Line
            while (strLine != null) {
                processedLines++;

                updateProgress(wave, processedLines, totalLines);

                if (strLine.contains(JRebirthMarkers.JREVENT.getName())) {
                    // Convert the string to a JRebirth event and add it to the list
                    addEvent(eventList, strLine.substring(strLine.indexOf(">>") + 2));
                }

                // Read the next line to process
                strLine = br.readLine();
            }

        } catch (final IOException e) {
            LOGGER.error("Error while processing event file", e);
        }
        return eventList;
    }
}
```

- `listen(DO_LOAD_EVENTS)` — registers the task with the Notifier (JIT during `initService()`).
- `doLoadEvents(File, Wave)` — runs on JTP; return value is sent on the return wave automatically.
- `updateMessage` / `updateProgress` — optional UI feedback linked to the source wave.


If you don't declare the return	**WaveType** a exception will be thrown at runtime when trying to send back the Service Task output.

If your Service Task doesn't return anything (void return) you can dismiss this step except if you want to receive an empty notification when the Job is done; just use *JRebirthItems.voidItem*.

You can also register your service task by putting **@OnWave** annoation using the action name of the **WaveType**. A best practice is to add annoation within interface methods, thus all implementations can avoid to use _listen_ methods.

**Alternative:** You can use the `@OnWave` annotation on the service interface method instead of manually calling `listen()`. This is the recommended approach as it keeps the contract visible in the interface definition.


```java
public interface MyService extends Service {

    WaveType DO_COMPUTE = WBuilder.waveType("COMPUTE")
        .items(MyWaves.INPUT)
        .returnAction("COMPUTED")
        .returnItem(MyWaves.OUTPUT);

    @OnWave("COMPUTE")
    @Priority(PriorityLevel.Normal)
    String doCompute(String input, Wave wave);
}
```

- `@OnWave("COMPUTE")` on the interface — impl skips `listen()` in `initService()`.
- Task method signature still defines parameters and return type for the contract checker.


Perform the Job!
-------------------


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.service.impl;

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.annotation.PriorityLevel;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.concurrent.Priority;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.facade.JRebirthEvent;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.module.Register;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.facade.JRebirthEventBase;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.log.JRebirthMarkers;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.service.DefaultService;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.service.ServiceUtility;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.service.LoadEdtFileService;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

/**
 * The class <strong>LoadEdtFileServiceImpl</strong> is the basic implementation used to load a log file.
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 */
@Register(value = LoadEdtFileService.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Low)
public class LoadEdtFileServiceImpl extends DefaultService implements LoadEdtFileService {

    /** The class logger. */
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(LoadEdtFileServiceImpl.class);

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void initService() {

        listen(DO_LOAD_EVENTS);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    @Priority(PriorityLevel.High)
    public List<JRebirthEvent> doLoadEvents(final File selecteFile, final Wave wave) {

        final List<JRebirthEvent> eventList = new ArrayList<>();

        updateMessage(wave, "Parsing events");

        // Get number of line to calculate the task progression
        final int totalLines = ServiceUtility.countFileLines(selecteFile);

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(selecteFile));) {
            int processedLines = 0;

            String strLine = br.readLine();

            // Read File Line By Line
            while (strLine != null) {
                processedLines++;

                updateProgress(wave, processedLines, totalLines);

                if (strLine.contains(JRebirthMarkers.JREVENT.getName())) {
                    // Convert the string to a JRebirth event and add it to the list
                    addEvent(eventList, strLine.substring(strLine.indexOf(">>") + 2));
                }

                // Read the next line to process
                strLine = br.readLine();
            }

        } catch (final IOException e) {
            LOGGER.error("Error while processing event file", e);
        }
        return eventList;
    }
}
```

- Return the task result from the method — framework wraps it into the return wave.
- Long loops should call `updateProgress(wave, current, total)` to keep UI responsive.


How to use the Service Feature
===================================

Follow these steps end-to-end:

1. Define service interface + `WaveType` (entry + return items).
2. Implement task method + `initService()` registration.
3. From Model: `returnData(...)` to start the task.
4. `listen(entry.returnWaveType())` on the caller.
5. Implement `doXxxCompleted(...)` handler to apply the result on JAT.

Call the Service
--------------------

To call this **Service Feature**, you can use the _returnData_ from any JRebirth's Component.
It takes at least 2 mandatory arguments:

1. The Service Class Object
2. The WaveType that is related to the Service Feature
3. An unordered list of WaveData objects that wrap values required by WaveType contract


Hereafter you will fin an example of service call with only one arguments passed:

```java
            returnData(LoadEdtFileService.class,
                       LoadEdtFileService.DO_LOAD_EVENTS,
                       WBuilder.waveData(EditorWaves.EVENTS_FILE, selected));
```

- `returnData(...)` — asynchronous service call; does not block the caller on JTP work.
- First argument — service interface class (facade lookup).
- Second argument — entry `WaveType` (`DO_LOAD_EVENTS`).
- `WBuilder.waveData(...)` — wraps the `File` input required by the wave contract.

Process the Service Result
--------------------------------

The Service Feature Result is sent as a Wave that wrap returned value. So to be informed when the result is available, there is two things to do:

* Let your component listening this WaveType.
* Add Wave handler code to process the result.


Each JRebirth's Component are able to listen some WaveType's waves by calling the **listen** method with one or several Wave Types.

This call must be done into one of the following method according to component used:

* *initCommand* : for Command classes
* *initService* : for Service classes
* *initModel* : for Model classes


Each initXXX method is called into JRebirth Internal Thread by ready method.


```java
    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        listen(LoadEdtFileService.DO_LOAD_EVENTS.returnWaveType());
        listen(EditorWaves.DO_UNLOAD);
    }
```

- `.returnWaveType()` — listen for the **completion** wave emitted when the service task finishes.
- Register in `initModel` / `initService` / `initCommand` — must run on JIT during component `ready`.

Add a method that suits the **WaveType** convention.

The name must used the predefined prefix (in our case DO_ converted to do), then the WaveType's name converted in a camel cased format.

Method parameters must be compliant with Wave Items defined into the Wave Type.

A final parameter must be added, the Wave itself taht could be useful to get extra data, for example when chained waves are used.


```java
    public void doEventsLoaded(final List<JRebirthEvent> eventList, final Wave wave) {
        view().activateButtons(!eventList.isEmpty());
    }
```

- `doEventsLoaded` — derived from return action `EVENTS_LOADED` on the service `WaveType`.
- First parameters — one per `WaveItem` on the return type (`List<JRebirthEvent>` here).
- `Wave wave` — always last; gives access to progress, chaining, or source context.

Threading
=============

Which Threads are involved ?
------------------------------

All communications with Service Component is done into **JIT** and all Service Tasks are performed 
into one slot of **JTP** or **HPTP** according to priority defined with annotation.

Threading Priority
-----------------------

Each Service feature call will be processed by the **JTP** (JRebirth Thread Pool) or by the **HPTP** (High Priority Thread Pool) according to their predefined priority.
By default the **Priority** is set to PriorityLevel.Low (a level below Normal) to let other task to be performed into JTP before Service feature calls (like Command).

Its possible to increase or decrease this value by adding an annotation on the Service Feature method like this:


```java
@Override
@Priority(PriorityLevel.High)
public List<JRebirthEvent> doLoadEvents(final File selectedFile, final Wave wave) {
    // High priority → prefer HPTP when JTP is saturated
}
```

- `@Priority(PriorityLevel.High)` — task may run on **HPTP** instead of default **JTP**.
- Default is `PriorityLevel.Low` — service tasks yield to commands and other JTP work.


### TaskTrackerService

JRebirth provides a built-in `TaskTrackerService` that monitors all running service tasks. It can be used to display a global progress indicator or to track background operations. Connect a UI component to the TaskTrackerService to show real-time task status across the entire application.


Follow Task progression
---------------------------

Each Service Task are able to update a progress bar with integer value and message.
In both cases you must provide the wave provided as a method argument, it will be used to link the associated **ServiceTask** and find the right **ProgressBar** and **Text** widgets to update.

You can update the message test by calling this method:


```java
updateMessage(wave, "Parsing events");
```

- `updateMessage(wave, text)` — updates linked status text for this task (if UI is wired).


You can update progress bar indicator by calling this method:


```java
updateProgress(wave, processedLines, totalLines);
```

- `updateProgress(wave, workDone, totalWork)` — drives an associated progress bar (0–1 internally).


If your Service Task returns void (no result to send back), use `JRebirthItems.voidItem` as the returnItem. This still generates a completion wave so listeners know the task finished.


```java
WaveType DO_CLEANUP = WBuilder.waveType("CLEANUP")
    .returnAction("CLEANUP_DONE")
    .returnItem(JRebirthItems.voidItem);

public void doCleanup(final Wave wave) { /* no return value */ }

public void doCleanupDone(final Wave wave) { /* task finished */ }
```

- `JRebirthItems.voidItem` — void return still emits a completion wave.
- Caller listens with `DO_CLEANUP.returnWaveType()` and implements `doCleanupDone(Wave)`.

